WEBVTT

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.740
Detections are only as effective as the telemetry supporting them.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:07.660
Security teams may use endpoint logs, authentication records, network events,

00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:12.230
cloud telemetry, application logs, email security events, and identity provider data.

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:15.440
Detection engineers need to understand what data exists, how it is collected,

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.936
how quickly it arrives, and what fields are available.

00:00:17.936 --> 00:00:22.280
A detection that depends on data the organization does not collect will fail no matter how

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:24.000
carefully the logic is written.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.500
Telemetry also has limitations.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:28.145
Logs may be incomplete, delayed, normalized differently,

00:00:28.145 --> 00:00:30.981
retained for different lengths of time, or missing important context.

00:00:30.981 --> 00:00:33.985
These constraints affect confidence and should be documented.

00:00:33.985 --> 00:00:37.270
Good visibility planning helps teams choose detections that are realistic for their

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:38.000
environment.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:43.040
It also exposes gaps that may require new logging, improved enrichment, better parsing,

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:45.910
or closer collaboration with infrastructure and application owners.
